    warning: cleartext-secrets: secrets go over plain http to a host that is not loopback: header Authorization

FINDINGS
HIGH (2)
  MOAT-T4-001  Remote server uses plain http://
    Server: web (claude-code, global)
    Where: url
    Risk: The remote server uses plain http:// to a host that is not loopback, so anyone on the network path can read or change the tool list, the prompts and any token sent to it.
    Fix: Use the https:// URL of the server. If the server has no TLS, do not use it over the network; run it locally or behind a TLS proxy.
  MOAT-T4-002  Plaintext secret in config
    Server: web (claude-code, global)
    Where: headers Authorization
    Risk: A secret is written in clear text in the client config, in the command arguments or in a URL. Anyone who can read the file, a backup or a shared dotfiles repo gets it. Command arguments also show in process listings, and URLs also show in logs.
    Fix: Move the secret out of the config: use an environment variable reference such as ${GITHUB_TOKEN} or a secret manager, and set the value outside the file. For a URL, send the secret in a header or an env reference instead of a query token or a path segment. Rotate the exposed secret.
